 -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the line $2x+y+1=0$ passes through the points $U(-1,1)$ and $V(0,-1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $U$ and $V$ under the action of $T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${U}^{′}(3,-3)$ and ${V}^{′}(0,1).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${U}^{′}$ and ${V}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-3) & =\\frac{1-(-3)}{0-3}(x-3) \\\\ y+3 & =-\\frac{4}{3}(x-3) \\\\ 3y+9 & =-4x+12 \\\\ 4x+3y+9 & =12 \\\\ 4x+3y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995865/state.json" "file_sha256" "d237c9fb2e1caaf4727a2be25f6a689a692e883c53df0a9fa599daabfb6ecedc"} {"question" "q-138787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995865/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c3c9a4c6f2f5a58520c6bbd075dd93cc9b1f98a16109382a4a04c2e30ad8abee"} {"question" "q-138787" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995865/state.json" "file_sha256" "d237c9fb2e1caaf4727a2be25f6a689a692e883c53df0a9fa599daabfb6ecedc"} {"question" "q-138787" "field" "field-1" "attr